  "answer_text": "Here is a full breakdown of the meaning of the word **INDOLENT**:\n\n---\n\n### \ud83d\udcd6 Definition\n\n**Indolent** is an adjective meaning **slow or lazy**. More specifically, it means **averse to activity, effort, or movement; habitually lazy**.\n\nAn indolent person is slow and lazy \u2014 not the type of person you'd want running your corporation or competing with you in a relay race.\n\n---\n\n### \ud83c\udfe5 Medical Usage\n\nDoctors also use the word *indolent* to describe medical conditions that are **slow to progress**. If you're diagnosed with an illness, you'd prefer an indolent one over one that spreads quickly.\n\n---\n\n### \ud83d\udd24 Part of Speech\n- **Adjective**\n- The noun form is **indolence** (e.g., laziness or the state of being idle)\n\n---\n\n### \ud83c\udf0d Etymology (Word Origin)\n\nThe word comes from **Latin** *indol\u0113ns*, meaning **\"not feeling pain\"**, from *in-* (not) + *dol\u0113re* (to grieve or cause distress). The main modern sense of **\"laziness, love of ease\"** dates to around 1710, perhaps reflecting the notion of avoiding trouble.\n\n---\n\n### \ud83d\udd01 Synonyms\nSynonyms include: **lazy, slack, idle, slow, sluggish, inactive, inert, languid, lethargic, listless, lackadaisical, torpid, slothful**.\n\n---\n\n### \u270f\ufe0f Example Sentences\n- *\"She was criticized for being indolent and irresponsible at work.\"*\n- *\"The indolent student rarely completed his assignments on time.\"*\n\nIn short, to call someone **indolent** is to say they are **unwilling to work, lazy, and lacking in energy or effort**.",
